{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_018y2ZrpR4C2Lr25tXMYLcgM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The error logs are clear. Both jobs fail at the same point - when trying to resolve `pymilvus<4.0,>=3.0.0` as a dependency of `langchain-milvus==0.4.0`. \n\n- x86_64: \"found 2 candidate(s) for pymilvus<4.0,>=3.0.0 but all were published within the last 3 days (release-age cooldown; oldest is 2 day(s) old)\"\n- aarch64: \"found no match for pymilvus<4.0,>=3.0.0 using PyPI resolver (searching at https://pypi.org/simple), searching for sdists, ignoring pre-release versions\"\nThe pipeline ran on July 20, 2026, and the x86_64 failure is due to the release-age cooldown - the matching pymilvus versions were only 2 days old when the job ran, but the cooldown requires 3 days. The aarch64 job likely encountered the same issue but with a different error message, possibly because it ran slightly later or the resolver behaves differently across architectures. So the x86_64 job found candidates but rejected them due to the release-age cooldown, while aarch64 found nothing at all. This discrepancy could mean the PyPI index changed between the two job runs, or aarch64 ran at a time when no matching versions were available yet. I'm wondering if there's a timing issue or if the version constraints are being resolved differently across the two architectures. pymilvus 3.x that satisfy the constraint, but aarch64 found nothing at all. This suggests the two architectures might be seeing different package availability, or aarch64 is hitting a different code path in fromager that returns \"found no match\" instead of the cooldown rejection message. match. The x86_64 resolver found 2 versions of pymilvus in the `<4.0,>=3.0.0` range but they're only 2 days old, hitting the 3-day release-age cooldown. The aarch64 resolver might be using a different code path that's specifically looking for sdists, and if only wheels