{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01KzewAu9eYAESUrTEXrcKDK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`langchain-milvus` is listed without a version pin in `team-notebooks-images.txt` for all rhai variants. The constraint is unversioned.\n\nNow let me check: when langchain-milvus resolves to 0.4.0, it requires `pymilvus<4.0,>=3.0.0`. This means pymilvus 3.x must have been released very recently (within the last 2-3 days). \n\nThe two key facts:\n1. `langchain-milvus 0.4.0` requires `pymilvus<4.0,>=3.0.0` (which means pymilvus 3.x)\n2. pymilvus 3.x is brand new, published just 2 days ago based on the x86_64 error\n\nI can either wait for the cooldown to expire—the pipeline ran on 2026-07-20 with a 3-day cooldown, so it should resolve by 2026-07-23 if pymilvus 3.x was published on 2026-07-18—or pin langchain-milvus to an older version like 0.1.x, 0.2.x, or 0.3.x that depends on pymilvus 2.x instead.\n\nLooking at the `rh-model-signing==1.0.3` example with its \"AIPCC-17310, cooldown bypass\" comment, it seems pinning to a specific version rather than allowing latest bypasses the cooldown mechanism. So I could either pin langchain-milvus to a version requiring pymilvus 2.x, or add a pymilvus constraint that avoids the cooldown entirely. I'm wondering if langchain-milvus 0.4.0 just released with pymilvus 3.x support, which would explain why the dependency resolution is failing. So pymilvus 3.x is the newly released version that triggered this whole issue. The aarch64 architecture is reporting \"no match\" even though x86_64 found pymilvus 3.x versions, which is puzzling since fromager builds from source and should be looking for sdists regardless of platform.\n\nThe timing suggests a possible race condition—x86_64 ran first and found candidates, but aarch64 ran a few minutes later and found nothing, possibly due to PyPI index changes or different resolver behavior between the two jobs. The real distinction seems to be that x86_64 hit a cooldown issue while aarch64 encountered a complete absence of matching packages. The aarch64 error specifically mentions searching for sdists, which suggests it's not finding any source distributions for pymilvus 3.x on PyPI. This points to a key difference: x86_64 might be counting wheel packages as candidates (which then fail the cooldown check), while aarch64's sdist-specific search comes up empty entirely. Though this raises a question about why fromager would treat these differently across architectures when it should consistently prefer sdists. Both architectures are hitting the same underlying issue: `langchain-milvus 0.4.0` requires `pymilvus>=3.0.0,<4.0`, but all versions in that range were published within the last 3 days, so Fromager's 3-day release-age cooldown is blocking them from being selected.\n\nI have three potential paths forward: wait until around July 23rd for the cooldown to expire, pin `pymilvus` to a specific version in the collection constraints to bypass the cooldown (similar to how the `rh-model-signing` example uses a pinned version), or downgrade `langchain-milvus` to a version that depends on `pymilvus 2.x` instead.\n\nThe pinning approach seems most practical—by specifying an exact version like `pymilvus==3.0.0`, fromager will look for that specific release rather than filtering through \"latest satisfying\" versions, which bypasses the cooldown restriction. The pymilvus 3.x versions do exist on PyPI and were published just before the cooldown window started, so they should be available once I confirm the exact latest 3.x release.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1408,"cache_read_input_tokens":72325,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1408,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"77a3f021-627e-47e4-9986-5fd96